    <div class="content" id="contentArea">
      <!-- ===== CARDS ===== -->
      <div id="vyapti-intro" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">1</span><h2>Vyāpti (व्याप्ति) — Invariable Concomitance</h2></div><p><strong>Vyāpti</strong> = The <strong>universal and invariable relationship</strong> between the hetu (reason) and sādhya (inferred). It is the <strong>foundation</strong> of inference (anumāna).</p><div class="formula-box">Vyāpti = Universal Relation between Hetu and Sādhya</div><p><strong>Key Points:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Vyāpti is the <strong>logical ground</strong> that makes inference possible.</li><li>It is a <strong>necessary relation</strong> — wherever hetu is present, sādhya must be present.</li><li>Without vyāpti, no valid inference can be drawn.</li><li>It is established through <strong>repeated observation</strong> (anvaya) and <strong>absence</strong> (vyatireka).</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Vyāpti:</strong> Wherever there is smoke, there is fire.</li><li>This universal relation allows us to infer fire from smoke.</li></ul></div>

      <div id="anvaya-vyatireka" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">2</span><h2>Anvaya & Vyatireka — अन्वय एवं व्यतिरेक</h2></div><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Anvaya (अन्वय) — Positive Concomitance:</strong> The positive relationship — wherever hetu is present, sādhya is also present. (e.g., Where there is smoke, there is fire.)</li><li><strong>Vyatireka (व्यतिरेक) — Negative Concomitance:</strong> The negative relationship — wherever sādhya is absent, hetu is also absent. (e.g., Where there is no fire, there is no smoke.)</li></ul><div class="formula-box">Anvaya = Presence of Hetu → Presence of Sādhya</div><div class="formula-box">Vyatireka = Absence of Sādhya → Absence of Hetu</div><p><strong>Example:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Anvaya:</strong> In a kitchen, there is smoke and fire (positive instance).</li><li><strong>Vyatireka:</strong> In a lake, there is no fire and no smoke (negative instance).</li></ul><p><strong>Together:</strong> Both anvaya and vyatireka establish vyāpti.</p></div>

      <div id="upadhi" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">3</span><h2>Upādhi (उपाधि) — Condition</h2></div><p><strong>Upādhi</strong> = A <strong>condition</strong> that restricts or limits the vyāpti between hetu and sādhya. It is a factor that must be present for the relation to hold.</p><div class="formula-box">Upādhi = Limiting Condition of Vyāpti</div><p><strong>Key Points:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>If there is an upādhi, the vyāpti is <strong>not universal</strong>.</li><li>A valid vyāpti must be <strong>free from upādhi</strong>.</li><li>Upādhi can <strong>vitiate</strong> (make invalid) the inference.</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>If we say "Wherever there is smoke, there is fire" — is there any condition? No, it's universal.</li><li>But if we say "Wherever there is a wet ground, it has rained" — there could be other conditions (sprinkler) → wet ground is not universally concomitant with rain.</li></ul></div>

      <div id="hetvabhasa-intro" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">4</span><h2>Hetvābhāsa (हेत्वाभास) — Fallacies of Reason</h2></div><p><strong>Hetvābhāsa</strong> = A <strong>fallacy of the hetu (reason)</strong> — when the reason appears to be valid but is actually <strong>invalid or defective</strong>. It is the Indian logical equivalent of a <strong>formal fallacy</strong>.</p><div class="formula-box">Hetvābhāsa = Defective/False Reason</div><p><strong>Five Types of Hetvābhāsa:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>1. Savyabhicāra / Anaikāntika — अनियत</strong></li><li><strong>2. Viruddha — विरुद्ध</strong></li><li><strong>3. Satpratipakṣa — सत्प्रतिपक्ष</strong></li><li><strong>4. Asiddha — असिद्ध</strong></li><li><strong>5. Bādhita — बाधित</strong></li></ul></div>

      <div id="savyabhicara" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">5</span><h2>Savyabhicāra / Anaikāntika (अनियत) — Irregular/Inconsistent</h2></div><p><strong>Savyabhicāra</strong> (or <strong>Anaikāntika</strong>) = The hetu is <strong>not uniformly</strong> related to the sādhya — it is <strong>too wide</strong> or <strong>inconsistent</strong>.</p><div class="formula-box">Savyabhicāra = Hetu present where Sādhya is absent</div><p><strong>Key Point:</strong> The hetu is <strong>not</strong> invariably concomitant with the sādhya.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Inferring "This is a swan because it is white" — but white things are not always swans (clouds, milk, etc. are white).</li><li>The hetu "whiteness" is present in many things other than swans → Savyabhicāra.</li></ul></div>

      <div id="viruddha" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">6</span><h2>Viruddha (विरुद्ध) — Contradictory</h2></div><p><strong>Viruddha</strong> = The hetu <strong>contradicts</strong> the sādhya — it proves the <strong>opposite</strong> of what is intended.</p><div class="formula-box">Viruddha = Hetu proves the Opposite of Sādhya</div><p><strong>Key Point:</strong> The hetu is <strong>inconsistent</strong> with the sādhya.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Inferring "This object is permanent because it is produced" — but produced things are impermanent.</li><li>The hetu "produced" actually proves the opposite (impermanence) → Viruddha.</li></ul></div>

      <div id="satpratipaksa" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">7</span><h2>Satpratipakṣa (सत्प्रतिपक्ष) — Counterbalanced</h2></div><p><strong>Satpratipakṣa</strong> = There is <strong>another equally valid</strong> reason that proves the <strong>opposite</strong> conclusion.</p><div class="formula-box">Satpratipakṣa = Equal Reason for Opposite Conclusion</div><p><strong>Key Point:</strong> The hetu is <strong>countered</strong> by another valid hetu.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Argument 1:</strong> Sound is eternal because it is perceptible (like atoms).</li><li><strong>Counter-argument:</strong> Sound is non-eternal because it is produced (like a pot).</li><li>Both are equally valid → Satpratipakṣa (neither is decisive).</li></ul></div>

      <div id="asiddha" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">8</span><h2>Asiddha (असिद्ध) — Unproved</h2></div><p><strong>Asiddha</strong> = The hetu itself is <strong>not established</strong> or <strong>not accepted</strong> — the reason is <strong>unproved</strong>.</p><div class="formula-box">Asiddha = Hetu is Unproved / Not Established</div><p><strong>Key Point:</strong> The hetu is <strong>not known</strong> to exist in the pakṣa.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Inferring "This hill has fire because it has smoke" — but if the hill is <strong>not known to have smoke</strong>, the hetu is asiddha.</li><li>Inferring "Sound is eternal because it is visible" — sound is not visible, so the hetu is unproved.</li></ul><p><strong>Sub-types:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Svarūpāsiddha:</strong> The hetu itself does not exist in the pakṣa.</li><li><strong>Viparyayāsiddha:</strong> The hetu exists but is not known.</li></ul></div>

      <div id="badhita" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">9</span><h2>Bādhita (बाधित) — Contradicted</h2></div><p><strong>Bādhita</strong> = The sādhya is <strong>contradicted</strong> by a <strong>stronger valid means of knowledge</strong> (pramāṇa).</p><div class="formula-box">Bādhita = Sādhya Contradicted by Stronger Pramāṇa</div><p><strong>Key Point:</strong> The conclusion is <strong>already known to be false</strong> by a stronger source of knowledge.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Inferring "Fire is cold because it is a substance" — but perception directly tells us fire is hot.</li><li>The sādhya (fire is cold) is contradicted by <strong>direct perception</strong> → Bādhita.</li></ul></div>

      <div id="comparison-table" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">10</span><h2>Comparison Table — तुलना तालिका</h2></div><table class="data-table"><tr><th>Hetvābhāsa</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Nature of Defect</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Savyabhicāra</strong></td><td>Irregular/Inconsistent</td><td>Hetu present where sādhya is absent</td><td>White → Swan (clouds are also white)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Viruddha</strong></td><td>Contradictory</td><td>Hetu proves opposite of sādhya</td><td>Produced → Permanent (produced proves impermanence)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Satpratipakṣa</strong></td><td>Counterbalanced</td><td>Equal reason for opposite conclusion</td><td>Perceptible → Eternal vs Produced → Non-eternal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Asiddha</strong></td><td>Unproved</td><td>Hetu itself is not established</td><td>Sound is eternal because it is visible (sound not visible)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bādhita</strong></td><td>Contradicted</td><td>Sādhya contradicted by stronger pramāṇa</td><td>Fire is cold (contradicted by perception)</td></tr></table></div>

      <div id="exam-traps" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">11</span><h2>Common Exam Traps — सामान्य परीक्षा जाल</h2></div><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Trap 1:</strong> Vyāpti = anvaya only → ❌ Vyāpti is established by both anvaya (positive) and vyatireka (negative).</li><li><strong>Trap 2:</strong> Upādhi = always a defect → ❌ Upādhi is a condition; if present, it makes vyāpti invalid, but it's not a fallacy itself.</li><li><strong>Trap 3:</strong> Savyabhicāra = contradictory → ❌ It is irregular/inconsistent, not contradictory (that's viruddha).</li><li><strong>Trap 4:</strong> Viruddha = counterbalanced → ❌ It is contradictory (proves opposite). Satpratipakṣa is counterbalanced.</li><li><strong>Trap 5:</strong> Asiddha = already contradicted → ❌ Asiddha = unproved; Bādhita = contradicted.</li><li><strong>Trap 6:</strong> Bādhita = unproved reason → ❌ Bādhita = conclusion contradicted by stronger pramāṇa.</li><li><strong>Trap 7:</strong> Satpratipakṣa = invalid inference → ❌ It means there is an equally valid counter-argument, not that the argument itself is invalid.</li></ul></div>

      <div id="practice-questions" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">12</span><h2>Practice Questions — अभ्यास प्रश्न</h2></div>
      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 1 (Vyāpti)</div>
      <p>Vyāpti is the relationship between:</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. Two pramāṇas</li><li>B. Hetu and Sādhya</li><li>C. Two pakṣas</li><li>D. Two vipakṣas</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: B. Hetu and Sādhya</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: Vyāpti is the invariable concomitance between the reason (hetu) and the inferred (sādhya).</p>

      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 2 (Savyabhicāra)</div>
      <p>"This is a swan because it is white" — what fallacy does this commit?</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. Viruddha</li><li>B. Savyabhicāra</li><li>C. Asiddha</li><li>D. Bādhita</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: B. Savyabhicāra</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: Whiteness is present in many things other than swans (clouds, milk) → irregular/inconsistent reason.</p>

      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 3 (Viruddha)</div>
      <p>"This object is permanent because it is produced" — what fallacy is this?</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. Savyabhicāra</li><li>B. Viruddha</li><li>C. Satpratipakṣa</li><li>D. Asiddha</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: B. Viruddha</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: "Produced" actually proves the opposite (impermanence) → contradictory reason.</p>

      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 4 (Satpratipakṣa)</div>
      <p>When there is an equally valid reason for the opposite conclusion, which hetvābhāsa occurs?</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. Viruddha</li><li>B. Satpratipakṣa</li><li>C. Asiddha</li><li>D. Bādhita</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: B. Satpratipakṣa</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: Satpratipakṣa is when an equally strong reason proves the opposite conclusion.</p>

      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 5 (Bādhita)</div>
      <p>"Fire is cold because it is a substance" — what fallacy is this?</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. Viruddha</li><li>B. Savyabhicāra</li><li>C. Bādhita</li><li>D. Asiddha</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: C. Bādhita</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: The conclusion "fire is cold" is contradicted by direct perception (which tells us fire is hot) → Bādhita.</p>

      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 6 (Anvaya & Vyatireka)</div>
      <p>The negative concomitance "Where there is no fire, there is no smoke" is called:</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. Anvaya</li><li>B. Vyatireka</li><li>C. Upādhi</li><li>D. Vyāpti</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: B. Vyatireka</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: Vyatireka is the negative concomitance — absence of sādhya implies absence of hetu.</p></div>

      <div id="one-page-revision" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">13</span><h2>One-Page Revision — एक पेज में पुनरावृत्ति</h2></div>
      <div class="formula-box">Vyāpti = Anvaya (Positive) + Vyatireka (Negative) = Invariable Concomitance</div>

      <p><strong>Key Terms:</strong></p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Vyāpti:</strong> Universal relation (Hetu ↔ Sādhya)</li>
      <li><strong>Anvaya:</strong> Positive concomitance (Hetu → Sādhya)</li>
      <li><strong>Vyatireka:</strong> Negative concomitance (No Sādhya → No Hetu)</li>
      <li><strong>Upādhi:</strong> Limiting condition of vyāpti</li></ul>

      <p><strong>Hetvābhāsa (5 Fallacies of Reason):</strong></p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Savyabhicāra (Anaikāntika):</strong> Hetu too wide/inconsistent</li>
      <li><strong>Viruddha:</strong> Hetu proves the opposite</li>
      <li><strong>Satpratipakṣa:</strong> Equal counter-reason</li>
      <li><strong>Asiddha:</strong> Hetu itself is unproved</li>
      <li><strong>Bādhita:</strong> Sādhya contradicted by stronger pramāṇa</li></ul>

      <p><strong>Exam Formula:</strong></p>
      <div class="formula-box">Vyāpti = Valid Reason | Hetvābhāsa = Defective/False Reason</div>
      <p><em>UGC NET में Vyāpti & Hetvābhāsa के questions identification, classification, और application पर based होते हैं।</em></p></div>

